  "title": "A crowd of 55 ants: two ways past a nestmate",
  "question": "Which colony reads as a satisfying, believable colony at work -- and is that difference worth changing the ants' default behaviour?",
  "context_md": "Same scene, same seed, same 240 frames starting at noon (frame 3600): 55 ants, nest at the left, a corpse pile 87 cells out. The only difference between the arms is one species flag.\n\nWhat is already measured (forage_probe seeds=8, the WP-4 instrument): one arm never gets an excursion past 14 cells and blocks ~61% of its moves; the other reaches 48-76 cells deep, puts 4-20 excursions per seed into the >=32 bucket, and blocks ~6.5%. Deliveries in that scene are zero in both arms (the pile is out of round-trip range at this horizon).\n\nOn the wetland colony scene (8 paired seeds, 3,834 frames) blocked moves fall in all 8 pairs, but deliveries moved: down in 4 seeds (the ones that mostly graze at the nest mouth), up in 2, tied in 2 -- median 62 vs 68 against a per-seed spread of 0-252. The economy is still the over-fed one (open bug L / the pending leaf-fall retune), so judge the *motion*, not the throughput.\n\nPlease ignore the day/night tint drift across the strip; both arms share it.",
